---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DateDifferenceCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/DateDifferenceCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="date-difference-calculator">
  <DateDifferenceCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to count the days between two dates</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the start date and the end date. The order does not matter; the tool swaps reversed dates.</li>
      <li>
        Decide if the end date counts. <strong>Include end date</strong> answers "how many days is this
        booking"; without it, the tool answers "how much time has passed".
      </li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Business days only</strong> to ignore Saturdays and Sundays.</li>
      <li>
        Turn on <strong>Include time of day</strong> when you need hours and minutes as well as whole days.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Inclusive or exclusive: the one decision that changes the answer</h2>
    <p>
      This causes most "the calculator is wrong" complaints, and it is almost always a question of definition,
      not arithmetic. Between 3 June and 5 June there are two days of elapsed time, but three days on the
      calendar. Both numbers are correct; they answer different questions.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Question</th>
            <th>Setting</th>
            <th>3 June to 5 June</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>How long until the deadline?</td>
            <td>Exclude end date</td>
            <td>2 days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>How many nights is the hotel stay?</td>
            <td>Exclude end date</td>
            <td>2 nights</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>How many days am I on holiday?</td>
            <td>Include end date</td>
            <td>3 days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>How many days of leave do I book?</td>
            <td>Include end date</td>
            <td>3 days</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Contracts and statutes usually state this explicitly. Phrases such as "clear days", "inclusive of both
      dates" or "from and including" exist because the default is ambiguous.
    </p>

    <h2>Why months are not 30 days</h2>
    <p>
      Calendar months have 28, 29, 30 or 31 days, so a "months between" figure needs a convention. This date
      difference calculator counts whole calendar months from the start date and reports the remaining days
      separately, as rent, notice periods and subscriptions work. It also shows the average-month approximation
      (total days divided by 30.436875, the mean Gregorian month) with a clear label, because some financial
      calculations need exactly that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The average year figure uses 365.2425 days, the length of the mean Gregorian year after the 4-year, 100-year
      and 400-year leap rules are averaged.
    </p>

    <h2>Business days here versus the business days calculator</h2>
    <p>
      The business-day count on this page removes only Saturdays and Sundays. That answers most quick questions,
      but SLAs and legal deadlines usually also exclude public holidays, and some regions do not use a
      Saturday–Sunday weekend. In those cases, use the business days calculator, where you can set the weekend days
      and keep your own holiday list.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Leap years follow the full Gregorian rule: divisible by 4, except centuries, except centuries divisible by
        400. The year 2000 was a leap year; 1900 and 2100 are not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Day counts use calendar dates, not timestamps, so a daylight-saving change inside the range cannot add or
        remove a day.
      </li>
      <li>
        With time of day on, hours and minutes are measured in the local timezone of your device, so a day that
        crosses a DST boundary is correctly 23 or 25 hours long.
      </li>
      <li>Dates before 1582 use the proleptic Gregorian calendar, as ISO 8601 specifies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Calculating notice periods, probation ends and contract terms.</li>
      <li>Counting days sober, days since a project started, or days until a wedding.</li>
      <li>Checking a 90-day visa window or a 30-day return policy.</li>
      <li>Invoice ageing: how many days past due an unpaid invoice is.</li>
      <li>Pregnancy and medical timelines given in weeks plus days.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
